[{:db/id 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c0d91061-b2d9-57bd-a897-ea0b99d019a6" "question-1f7a0341-d787-5ede-9a89-8dbc8c2dee11"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184112 :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=81$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each $x_i$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nWe're given that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180$ and $n=81.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81-1}\\cdot 180 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}\\cdot 180 \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = 2.25.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\sigma} = \\sqrt{2.25} = 1.5.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184195 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4022c5eb-3ae0-5385-a3a9-5931c88f8e95" "question-41998970-961a-5e8f-b06c-92a60dd861ec" "question-5dc7d792-7eaf-56a2-b4c9-87a9e26c63c4" "question-4b2fa2e2-e3ba-5e1d-bc8c-1949a8b239ed" "question-4e0511a4-2bea-5713-96a8-6cab28985040"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184093 :question/problem "Suppose that $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_9$ is a random sample of size $n=9$ from a population, and \n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nCompute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nHere, there are $n=9$ data points in the sample, and we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{8} \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184194 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f7daca4-074f-50e6-8f6a-0126ae82d27d" "question-9d145845-d1fd-557d-b5dc-7262bedf9b90" "question-6bf56380-19c5-5109-bbec-843d8876747c" "question-c16ee770-6755-511d-a2bf-8ccbcb77f364"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184092 :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n5, \\quad 3, \\quad 6, \\quad 4, \\quad 7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes the unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(5+3+6+4+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{135}{5} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =27-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $2.5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c0d91061-b2d9-57bd-a897-ea0b99d019a6" :question/id #uuid "c0d91061-b2d9-57bd-a897-ea0b99d019a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139847" :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=11$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=220\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each ${x}_{i}$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=220$ and $n=11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11-1}\\cdot 220 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 220 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $22.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a76954e-73f1-5964-bd22-6c22516b62a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a76954e-73f1-5964-bd22-6c22516b62a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a76954e-73f1-5964-bd22-6c22516b62a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f99ee14-f921-55bb-beae-94d55f4763f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6f4d205-ccff-5bbf-b86d-955d5121dea6" "answer-7682beb4-209c-5c8e-b02c-5574fd9c52a6" "answer-a8bb74fe-3173-5959-9647-a075edc932ae" "answer-5f99ee14-f921-55bb-beae