data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{265}{5} \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =53-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186183829 :answer-field/correct 17592186183832 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f7daca4-074f-50e6-8f6a-0126ae82d27d" :question/id #uuid "2f7daca4-074f-50e6-8f6a-0126ae82d27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-89000" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n19,\\,11,\\,20,\\,14,\\,16\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(19+11+20+14+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({19}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{20}^{2}+{14}^{2}+{16}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,334}{5} \\\\  & =266.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =266.8-{16}^{2} \\\\  & =10.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 10.8 \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{4} \\\\  & =13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $13.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7c916ff-d433-5b36-a6c0-190697c1b089"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7c916ff-d433-5b36-a6c0-190697c1b089" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c916ff-d433-5b36-a6c0-190697c1b089" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf85e7e1-51e9-5c34-9e99-adf0e1b11997" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6abbcb5-083f-5afd-b9e1-d8bdcfede116" "answer-65e4a95b-42ab-5a2c-99dd-38bc188bce86" "answer-cadf2674-531a-5eb7-beb3-ef85205e6e70" "answer-cf85e7e1-51e9-5c34-9e99-adf0e1b11997" "answer-070b0ec1-e144-5414-93ef-c5d5462a2e0d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6abbcb5-083f-5afd-b9e1-d8bdcfede116" :answer/id #uuid "f6abbcb5-083f-5afd-b9e1-d8bdcfede116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65e4a95b-42ab-5a2c-99dd-38bc188bce86" :answer/id #uuid "65e4a95b-42ab-5a2c-99dd-38bc188bce86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cadf2674-531a-5eb7-beb3-ef85205e6e70" :answer/id #uuid "cadf2674-531a-5eb7-beb3-ef85205e6e70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf85e7e1-51e9-5c34-9e99-adf0e1b11997" :answer/id #uuid "cf85e7e1-51e9-5c34-9e99-adf0e1b11997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-070b0ec1-e144-5414-93ef-c5d5462a2e0d" :answer/id #uuid "070b0ec1-e144-5414-93ef-c5d5462a2e0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d145845-d1fd-557d-b5dc-7262bedf9b90" :question/id #uuid "9d145845-d1fd-557d-b5dc-7262bedf9b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-89001" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n-6,\\,7,\\,10,\\,-9,\\,8\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(-6+7+10-9+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}[(-6)^{2}+{7}^{2}+{10}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{8}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{330}{5} \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =66-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 62 \\\\  & =\\frac{310}{4} \\\\  & =77.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $77.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-86777429-62e1-5a84-a748-657f0d5246f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-86777429-62e1-5a84-a748-657f0d5246f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "86777429-62e1-5a84-a748-657f0d5246f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff54205f-411e-56f3-aefd-b6d54fdc08ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3009f909-e476-50ba-a047-197b6a85673b" "answer-c5252f71-8ab7-57cf-b784-74a395cd1b58" "answer-e38e698d-13ff-5433-b5e1-e34e1dad7c6f" "answer-63de4533-90d1-5673-94b5-dca8efe8b5f7" "answer-ff54205f-411e-56f3-aefd-b6d54fdc08ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3009f909-e476-50ba-a047-197b6a85673b" :answer/id #uuid "3009f909-e476-50ba-a047-197b6a85673b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "62.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5252f71-8ab7-57cf-b784-74a395cd1b58" :answer/id #uuid "c5252f71-8ab7-57cf-b784-74a395cd1b58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e38e698d-13ff-5433-b5e1-e34e1dad7c6f" :answer/id #uuid "e38e698d-13ff-5433-b5e1-e34e1dad7c6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63de4533-90d1-5673-94b5-dca8efe8b5f7" :answer/id #uuid "63de4533-90d1-5673-94b5-dca8efe8b5f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "71.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff54205f-411e-56f3-aefd-b6d54fdc08ff" :answer/id #uuid "ff54205f-411e-56f3-aefd-b6d54fdc08ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "77.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6bf56380-19c5-5109-bbec-843d8876747c" :question/id #uuid "6bf56380-19c5-5109-bbec-843d8876747c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89005" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n19,\\,-14,\\,22,\\,-21\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(19-14+22-21) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4}({19}^{2}+(-14)^{2}+{22}^{2}+(-21)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,482}{4} \\\\  & =370.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =370.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =368.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4-1}\\cdot 368.25 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,473}{3} \\\\  & =491.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $491.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-956bf479-cc80-5030-9e9d-9a71953b58b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-956bf479-cc80-5030-9e9d-9a71953b58b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "956bf479-cc80-5030-9e9d-9a71953b58b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eeadfdd3-fe19-5081-8c37-4dd3c1a51682" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ded2ab9e-49d1-50b4-bd11-0a2b7bf29c63" "answer-f032a8ee-7fd6-5939-b4db-7b2013dfef22" "answer-a03f5f55-1043-59c3-b81a-0992d5c66d03" "answer-eeadfdd3-fe19-5081-8c37-4dd3c1a51682" "answer-b7121738-8476-5e5d-b9c6-7535b443a0e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ded2ab9e-49d1-50b4-bd11-0a2b7bf29c63" :answer/id #uuid "ded2ab9e-49d1-50b4-bd11-0a2b7bf29c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "368" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f032a8ee-7fd6-5939-b4db-7b2013dfef22" :answer/id #uuid "f032a8ee-7fd6-5939-b4db-7b2013dfef22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "370" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a03f5f55-1043-59c3-b81a-0992d5c66d03" :answer/id #uuid "a03f5f55-1043-59c3-b81a-0992d5c66d03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "490" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeadfdd3-fe19-5081-8c37-4dd3c1a51682" :answer/id #uuid "eeadfdd3-fe19-5081-8c37-4dd3c1a51682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "491" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7121738-8476-5e5d-b9c6-7535b443a0e0" :answer/id #uuid "b7121738-8476-5e5d-b9c6-7535b443a0e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "497" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c16ee770-6755-511d-a2bf-8ccbcb77f364" :question/id #uuid "c16ee770-6755-511d-a2bf-8ccbcb77f364" :question/math-academy-id "q-88999" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n13,\\,15,\\,9,\\,11,\\,12\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(13+15+9+11+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({13}^{2}+{15}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{12}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{740}{5} \\\\  & =148.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =148-{12}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88e4a68e-c7a0-55f8-9072-f9288e94c7b8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88e4a68e-c7a0-55f8-9072-f9288e94c7b8" :answer-field/id #uuid "88e4a68e-c7a0-55f8-9072-f9288e94c7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fb745932-05e5-5ac3-8346-680974ad3332" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15c9d4c7-4932-51cb-851e-20bc41eff0b1" "answer-cf6f6514-8524-5db9-8a22-6714044d22e1" "answer-ed063622-78e8-5cc4-9cae-11117d2078f5" "answer-fb745932-05e5-5ac3-8346-680974ad3332" "answer-6d11e664-ae79-5311-b5f7-4596a10ca2c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15c9d4c7-4932-51cb-851e-20bc41eff0b1" :answer/id #uuid "15c9d4c7-4932-51cb-851e-20bc41eff0b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf6f6514-8524-5db9-8a22-6714044d22e1" :answer/id #uuid "cf6f6514-8524-5db9-8a22-6714044d22e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed063622-78e8-5cc4-9cae-11117d2078f5" :answer/id #uuid "ed063622-78e8-5cc4-9cae-11117d2078f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb745932-05e5-5ac3-8346-680974ad3332" :answer/id #uuid "fb745932-05e5-5ac3-8346-680974ad3332" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d11e664-ae79-5311-b5f7-4596a10ca2c3" :answer/id #uuid "6d11e664-ae79-5311-b5f7-4596a10ca2c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185357 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nWe've already seen that the sample mean $\\overline{X},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator of $\\mu,$ which means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nThe definition of the sample mean follows naturally from the definition of $\\mu.$\n\nHowever, when constructing estimators for the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ things are not so simple! \n\nFrom the definition of the population variance, you might expect that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator for $\\sigma^2.$ However, it can be shown that it is a *biased* estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\\right] \\neq \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nThis might seem surprising, but it's true. \n\nTo compute an *unbiased* estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we use the so-called **sample variance**. The sample variance is denoted $S^2,$ and is defined as\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we will prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Notice that instead of dividing by $n$ (as with our original biased estimator), we instead divide by $n-1.$ This is sometimes referred to as **Bessel's correction**, as it corrects the bias of the original estimator.\n\n- We should think of $S^2$ as a random variable whose value varies according to the particular sample under consideration.\n\n- Since $S^2$ is a random variable, it has a sampling distribution (i.e., a probability distribution). We'll learn more about the sampling distribution of $S^2$ in future lessons.\n\nFinally, we have an alternative formula for the sample variance that's often more convenient in practice:\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185358 :tutorial/content "Here, we will show that\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right].\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses and distributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}^{2}-2{X}_{i}\\overset{―}{X}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2{X}_{i}\\overset{―}{X}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-2\\overset{―}{X}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider each sum separately:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 = n\\cdot \\overline{X^2}$ follows directly from the  definition of the mean of $X^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i = n\\cdot \\overline{X}$ follows directly from the  definition of the mean of $X$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\, \\textrm{times}} = n$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-2\\overset{―}{X}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[n\\cdot \\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-2\\overset{―}{X}\\cdot n\\cdot \\overset{―}{X}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot n] \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-2{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185326 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a sample of size $n,$ the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2.\n$$\n\nWe will now prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ that is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[S^2\\right] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nA major step towards this goal is to find the expected value of the sum of squares term:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 \\right] \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFirst, consider the following sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this term by adding and subtracting the sample mean $\\overline X,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left((X_i - \\overline X) + (\\overline X -  \\mu)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSquaring and applying the laws of summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ))}^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}) & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+n(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have used the fact that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \\overline X) = 0$ and that $(\\overline X - \\mu)$ does not depend on $i$. Thus, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 =  \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 + n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 -  n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2  . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast\\ast)\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is the summation term $(\\ast)$ we wish to find the expectation of.\n\nNow, we make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}] & ={\\sigma }^{2} \\\\ \\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] & =\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding the expectation of $(\\ast\\ast),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}]-n\\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-n\\cdot \\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n} \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}]=\\frac{(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the statistic\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nis a *biased* estimate of $\\sigma^2.$ Therefore, to form an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we must divide our summation term by $n-1,$ not $n.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{S}^{2}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}\\cdot (n-1){\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184405 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
